--TEST--
every `#[Command]` and `#[Option]` the program declares is readable back out of its own declaration, so a usage text is generated rather than written — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`, `rule:attributes/structural-retrieval` and `rule:attributes/retrieval-folds-while-checking`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`'s command table is built while compiling, and its reader —
// `Core\Command::help(?string $name): Cli\Text` — is goal `core-part-ii`'s, because a
// terminal is not reachable before capabilities exist
// (docs/agent/goals/core-depth.md item 6). So this case asks the question
// that *is* answerable today, and it is the one that section's promise rests
// on: **nobody writes usage text**, which is only true if every word of it is
// already in the declaration. `rule:attributes/structural-retrieval` and `rule:attributes/retrieval-folds-while-checking`'s retrieval reads those payloads
// back at the call site, so the help below contains no text of its own —
// every name, every spelling and every sentence in it was retrieved.
//
// Two things are asserted, and the first is not a line below:
//
//   * **The table is the compilation.** § 6's three compile errors — a
//     duplicate command name, two options sharing a spelling, an `#[Option]`
//     on a parameter whose declared type has no conversion from `string` —
//     are all reachable from this file, so a table that never grew a row, or
//     grew two rows for one name, does not reach the runtime at all.
//   * **Correctness is counted.** Four rows over three methods, every one of
//     them described, no two named alike — a retrieval that answered
//     plausibly for `deploy` and collapsed the two names one method declares
//     still fails the sweep.
//
// The `about` is asked of a command and of an option through *one* shape,
// which is the agreement this case is built around: § 6 gives both kinds of
// row the same field, so a declaration that grew its own spelling for one of
// them fails here while still reading correctly on its own line.

// § 6's own example, written as that section writes it: a parameter is a
// positional argument unless it carries `#[Option]`, with no inference from
// defaults or types, and `uint $retries` is converted during matching.
class Deploy {
    #[Core\Command(name: "deploy", about: "Push the current build")]
    public static function deploy(
        tainted string $target,
        #[Core\Option(short: "n", long: "dry-run", about: "Print what would happen")] bool $dryRun,
        #[Core\Option] uint $retries = 3,
    ): uint {
        echo $target, $dryRun ? "!" : "", $retries, "\n";
        return 0;
    }
}

// A second class, because the table is built out of `rule:programs/implementing`'s
// whole-program enumeration rather than out of one declaration site — and a
// second option carrying an `about` and no `short`, which is the other side of
// the bound the counters below name.
class Migrate {
    #[Core\Command(name: "migrate", about: "Apply pending migrations")]
    public static function migrate(
        #[Core\Option(about: "Say what would happen and stop")] bool $pretend,
    ): void {
    }
}

// `rule:attributes/repeatable`'s repetition, read as `nvs_types::commands` reads it: two
// `#[Command]`s on one method are two names for one implementation, which is
// what an alias is. Both become rows, so the roster below is four long over
// three methods.
class Seed {
    #[Core\Command(name: "seed", about: "Load the fixtures")]
    #[Core\Command(name: "fixtures", about: "Load the fixtures")]
    public static function seed(): void {
    }
}

// The one place a retrieval's `null` is turned into a countable answer. A
// `?T` narrows inside an `if` body and nowhere else, so without these the
// sweep below would be written as nested `if`s and the help would be written
// as text.
class Help {
    public static function name(?{name: string} $row): string {
        if ($row == null) { return ""; }
        return $row->name;
    }

    // Asked of a `#[Command]` and of an `#[Option]` alike — see the header.
    public static function about(?{about: string} $row): string {
        if ($row == null) { return ""; }
        return $row->about;
    }

    // The short spelling is optional, so its absence is the empty string and
    // the rendered line simply has no `-x, ` in front of its long form.
    public static function short(?{short: string} $row): string {
        if ($row == null) { return ""; }
        return "-" . $row->short . ", ";
    }

    public static function long(?{long: string} $row): string {
        if ($row == null) { return ""; }
        return "--" . $row->long;
    }
}

// § 4's method spelling: a method reference names the method the
// `#[Command]` is attached to.
?{name: string} $deployName = Core\Attributes::get<{name: string}>(Deploy::deploy(...));
?{about: string} $deployAbout = Core\Attributes::get<{about: string}>(Deploy::deploy(...));
?{name: string} $migrateName = Core\Attributes::get<{name: string}>(Migrate::migrate(...));
?{about: string} $migrateAbout = Core\Attributes::get<{about: string}>(Migrate::migrate(...));

// `all<T>` rather than `get<T>`, because this method declares two rows and
// `get<T>` answers the first — the alias is invisible to the singular form.
array<{name: string}> $seedNames = Core\Attributes::all<{name: string}>(Seed::seed(...));
array<{about: string}> $seedAbouts = Core\Attributes::all<{about: string}>(Seed::seed(...));

array<string> $names = [
    Help::name($deployName),
    Help::name($migrateName),
    $seedNames[0]->name,
    $seedNames[1]->name,
];

array<string> $abouts = [
    Help::about($deployAbout),
    Help::about($migrateAbout),
    $seedAbouts[0]->about,
    $seedAbouts[1]->about,
];

int $described = 0;
foreach ($abouts as string $one) {
    if ($one != "") { $described = $described + 1; }
}

int $unique = 0;
foreach ($names as string $one) {
    int $same = 0;
    foreach ($names as string $other) {
        if ($one == $other) { $same = $same + 1; }
    }
    if ($same == 1) { $unique = $unique + 1; }
}

echo "commands=", Core\Arr::count($names), "\n";
echo "described=", $described, "\n";
echo "unique=", $unique, "\n";

// § 4's parameter spelling: the same reference plus the parameter's own name,
// written as a literal. The three options are asked the one `about` shape the
// two commands above were asked, and the two that carry a sentence answer it.
?{about: string} $dryRunAbout = Core\Attributes::get<{about: string}>(Deploy::deploy(...), "dryRun");
?{about: string} $retriesAbout = Core\Attributes::get<{about: string}>(Deploy::deploy(...), "retries");
?{about: string} $pretendAbout = Core\Attributes::get<{about: string}>(Migrate::migrate(...), "pretend");
?{short: string} $dryRunShort = Core\Attributes::get<{short: string}>(Deploy::deploy(...), "dryRun");
?{short: string} $pretendShort = Core\Attributes::get<{short: string}>(Migrate::migrate(...), "pretend");
?{long: string} $dryRunLong = Core\Attributes::get<{long: string}>(Deploy::deploy(...), "dryRun");

array<string> $optionAbouts = [
    Help::about($dryRunAbout),
    Help::about($retriesAbout),
    Help::about($pretendAbout),
];

int $documented = 0;
foreach ($optionAbouts as string $one) {
    if ($one != "") { $documented = $documented + 1; }
}

int $shorts = 0;
if (Help::short($dryRunShort) != "") { $shorts = $shorts + 1; }
if (Help::short($pretendShort) != "") { $shorts = $shorts + 1; }

echo "options=", Core\Arr::count($optionAbouts), "\n";
echo "documented=", $documented, "\n";
echo "shorts=", $shorts, "\n";

// The one thing retrieval cannot see, said out loud rather than left as a
// surprising count: it is *structural*, so a payload-less `#[Option]` and a
// parameter carrying no attribute at all answer the same nothing. `$target`
// is positional and `$retries` is an option with no fields, and both are
// `none` here. § 6's table tells them apart because it matches the attribute
// *nominally* off `nvs_types::derive::ATTRIBUTES` — which is the whole reason
// that roster exists, and is why the table is not built out of this pass.
?{about: string} $targetAbout = Core\Attributes::get<{about: string}>(Deploy::deploy(...), "target");
echo "positional=", $targetAbout != null ? "some" : "none", "\n";
echo "bare option=", $retriesAbout != null ? "some" : "none", "\n";

// The help itself. Every word below came out of a declaration; the layout is
// all this file contributes, and § 6's generated version replaces even that.
echo "\n";
echo Help::name($deployName), " - ", Help::about($deployAbout), "\n";
echo "  ", Help::short($dryRunShort), Help::long($dryRunLong), "  ", Help::about($dryRunAbout), "\n";
echo Help::name($migrateName), " - ", Help::about($migrateAbout), "\n";
echo "  ", Help::short($pretendShort), Help::about($pretendAbout), "\n";
echo $seedNames[0]->name, " - ", $seedAbouts[0]->about, "\n";
echo $seedNames[1]->name, " - ", $seedAbouts[1]->about, "\n";
--EXPECT--
commands=4
described=4
unique=4
options=3
documented=2
shorts=1
positional=none
bare option=none

deploy - Push the current build
  -n, --dry-run  Print what would happen
migrate - Apply pending migrations
  Say what would happen and stop
seed - Load the fixtures
fixtures - Load the fixtures
